item.text:
**Reused evidence**
None of the previously accepted evidence IDs were a clean reuse for Chapter 5B.

**New evidence**
- Buhari framed the 2022 budget as a growth-and-sustainability package, with gender-responsive budgeting and explicit intent to distribute resources more fairly.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 BUDGET SPEECH: (Budget of Economic Growth and Sustainability) Delivered by President Muhammadu Buhari at the Joint Session of the National Assembly, Abuja","publisher":"Federal Ministry of Information and National Orientation","publication_date":"2022-01-??","url":"https://fmino.gov.ng/2022-budget-speech-budget-of-economic-growth-and-sustainability-delivered-by-president-muhammadu-buhari-at-the-joint-session-of-the-national-assembly-abuja/","claim":"Buhari said the 2022 budget was the first in Nigeria's history to explicitly advise MDAs on gender-responsive budgeting and described it as a step toward fairer resource distribution and support for vulnerable groups.","locator":"paragraph 37 / lines 245-245","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential budget speech; authoritative for stated intent and formal priorities, though self-presenting.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 budget-setting period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari action and intent in a formal budget speech.","contrary_evidence":["Rhetorical commitment does not prove implementation","later fiscal results were weak"],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.6","5B.8"]}

- The same budget speech emphasized diversification, infrastructure, health, education, poverty reduction, and reducing regional disparities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"2022 BUDGET SPEECH: (Budget of Economic Growth and Sustainability) Delivered by President Muhammadu Buhari at the Joint Session of the National Assembly, Abuja","publisher":"Federal Ministry of Information and National Orientation","publication_date":"2022-01-??","url":"https://fmino.gov.ng/2022-budget-speech-budget-of-economic-growth-and-sustainability-delivered-by-president-muhammadu-buhari-at-the-joint-session-of-the-national-assembly-abuja/","claim":"Buhari said the 2022 budget was guided by diversification, infrastructure investment, better health and education, poverty reduction, and minimizing regional, economic, and social disparities, and that loans would finance roads, rail, power, water, irrigation, and health projects.","locator":"paragraphs 34, 49-52, 58-59 / lines 235-245 and 272-296","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct speech with concrete fiscal and investment priorities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 budget and implementation framing.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari policy agenda, though spending execution depended on ministries and borrowing conditions.","contrary_evidence":["Planned priorities were not fully realized in execution","heavy borrowing remained necessary"],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.3","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- The Central Bank's fiscal-policy page shows the 2022 budget was passed and aligned to the National Development Plan objectives.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fiscal Policy","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/MonetaryPolicy/Fiscal.html","claim":"The CBN says the 2022 budget was passed and assented to in April 2022 and was tied to the 2021-2025 National Development Plan goals of diversification, infrastructure, security, education, health, poverty reduction, and reducing disparities.","locator":"section 'THE 2022 BUDGET', lines 10-13","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official fiscal summary, dated later but explicitly describing 2022 budget structure and priorities.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 budget year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but strong: this is the fiscal framework Buhari approved and his administration executed.","contrary_evidence":["Describes formal budget design more than outcomes","does not by itself show effective implementation"],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.6"]}

- The same CBN page shows weak fiscal execution: low revenue, under-executed capital spending, and borrowing-financed deficits in 2022.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Fiscal Policy","publisher":"Central Bank of Nigeria","publication_date":"2026-07-22","url":"https://www.cbn.gov.ng/MonetaryPolicy/Fiscal.html","claim":"The CBN says 2022 total revenue was below budget, capital expenditure was far below plan, and the deficit was financed largely through domestic borrowing and ways-and-means advances; total public debt rose to N46.25 trillion by end-2022.","locator":"lines 14-23","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official summary with concrete budget-performance numbers and year-end debt data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 execution year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct on Buhari-era fiscal execution, though the borrowing and debt management were partly mediated by finance and monetary institutions.","contrary_evidence":["The page still shows the budget framework existed and capital projects were prioritized on paper"],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.6","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- IMF said the 2022 oil-price windfall created a fiscal opportunity that Nigeria did not harness, while inflation stayed elevated and fuel subsidies drained revenue.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/nigeria-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF said higher oil prices created an opportunity to build fiscal space and FX reserves, but that opportunity was not harnessed; inflation was elevated and fuel subsidies remained a formidable drain on fiscal revenues.","locator":"lines 239-240","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Independent multilateral assessment with explicit macro-fiscal judgment and 2022 timing.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 macroeconomic conditions.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Buhari-era macro policy, though constrained by oil theft, global prices, and inherited weaknesses.","contrary_evidence":["Nigeria still had positive growth and some revenue gains","the IMF frames the issue as policy choice under constraint"],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.7","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- IMF also credited the authorities with some tax-administration work, including TaxPro Max, while urging deeper reforms.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/nigeria-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF welcomed the steady implementation of the TaxPro Max tax automation system and urged broader tax-administration reforms, including better segmentation, customs modernization, and PAYE administration.","locator":"lines 251-254","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct, specific policy assessment by the IMF mission.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 reform context.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Buhari administration's revenue apparatus and fiscal team.","contrary_evidence":["The IMF presents this as partial progress rather than a completed reform","larger revenue gaps remained"],"lenses":["5B.5","5B.7"]}

- IMF said monetary policy had tightened, but conditions remained accommodative because deficit financing and directed lending were still large.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria: Staff Concluding Statement of the 2022 Article IV Mission","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-18","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/18/nigeria-staff-concluding-statement-of-the-2022-article-iv-mission","claim":"The IMF said the CBN had raised the policy rate by a cumulative 400 basis points, but monetary conditions were still accommodative because budget financing through the CBN and directed lending programs continued to expand money supply.","locator":"lines 261-266","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific policy and macro-finance assessment with explicit numeric details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 monetary-policy year.","ruler_attribution":"Direct to Buhari-era monetary governance through the CBN, though operationally executed by the central bank.","contrary_evidence":["Rate hikes show some stabilization effort","the IMF still judged the stance insufficient"],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.5","5B.7","5B.9"]}

- World Bank said Nigeria's subsidy regime was regressive and crowded out pro-poor spending.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"To Promote Inclusive and Sustainable Development, Nigeria Needs to Fix its Public Finances","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/11/21/to-promote-inclusive-and-sustainable-development-nigeria-needs-to-fix-its-public-finances","claim":"The World Bank said petrol, electricity, and FX subsidies were inefficient and regressive, benefited primarily wealthy households, distorted incentives, discouraged investment, and crowded out pro-poor programs.","locator":"lines 186-192","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank press release summarizing a country-specific public finance review with concrete distributional claims.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 fiscal debate and year-end assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Directly relevant to Buhari-era policy, especially subsidy and FX management.","contrary_evidence":["The government had long defended subsidies as social protection","the source is a policy critique rather than a neutral outcome table"],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.5","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- The same World Bank review said Nigeria had among the world's lowest spending and revenue ratios and that reforms would improve the business environment and FDI.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"To Promote Inclusive and Sustainable Development, Nigeria Needs to Fix its Public Finances","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-11-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/11/21/to-promote-inclusive-and-sustainable-development-nigeria-needs-to-fix-its-public-finances","claim":"The World Bank said Nigeria's public spending averaged 12 percent of GDP and revenue about 7 percent of GDP from 2015 to 2021, and argued that fiscal reform, a market-based exchange rate, and trade/tax rationalization would improve the business environment and attract FDI.","locator":"lines 188-194","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Specific quantitative macro-fiscal comparison from a major multilateral review.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Strong background for 2022 policy choices.","ruler_attribution":"Relevant to Buhari's policy environment; not all figures are exclusively attributable to him, but the 2022 choices are part of the same regime.", "contrary_evidence":["Low spending/revenue are partly structural and inherited","the report is prescriptive, not a causal identification"],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.4","5B.6","5B.10"]}

- The World Bank poverty assessment emphasized poor welfare-data frequency, labor-market and social-protection analysis, and major state-level disparities.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Poverty Assessment","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-03-22","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/nigeria/publication/afw-nigeria-poverty-assessment","claim":"The World Bank said microdata for welfare and poverty analysis are far less frequent than macro data, introduced new analysis on labor markets, climate shocks, and social protection, and stressed that outcomes differ dramatically across Nigerian states, especially north versus south.","locator":"lines 41-53","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank country assessment with clear distributional framing, though the page is high-level rather than the full report.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 contextual baseline.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly context rather than direct Buhari attribution, but it frames distributional constraints his government faced.","contrary_evidence":["It describes structural data limitations more than a Buhari choice","state disparities predate 2022"],"lenses":["5B.6","5B.8","5B.10"]}

- NBS reported inflation still above 21 percent at end-2022, showing continued price pressure under Buhari.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"CPI and Inflation Report December 2022","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241274","claim":"NBS reported headline inflation of 21.34 percent in December 2022, up 5.72 percentage points year on year from December 2021, with the 12-month average CPI also rising further.","locator":"executive summary lines 46-52","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"National statistical release with clear numeric time series.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact December 2022 outcome.","ruler_attribution":"Outcome under Buhari's 2022 macroeconomic management, though influenced by global food and fuel shocks.","contrary_evidence":["Inflation eased slightly month to month in December","some of the pressure was externally driven"],"lenses":["5B.3","5B.9","5B.10"]}

- NBS labor-force data showed underemployment and weak hours worked, with women, youth, the less educated, and rural residents most affected.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Labour Force Survey Q4 2022 & Q1 2023","publisher":"National Bureau of Statistics","publication_date":"2023","url":"https://nigerianstat.gov.ng/elibrary/read/1241365","claim":"NBS reported that 36.4 percent of employed persons worked fewer than 40 hours per week in Q4 2022, underemployment was 13.7 percent, and short-hours work was most common among women, younger people, those with less education, and rural residents.","locator":"lines 46-57","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official labor-force release with a clear household/employment distribution pattern.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly overlaps Q4 2022 and is close to the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Contextual to Buhari-era labor conditions, not a single ruler action, but relevant to distributional performance.","contrary_evidence":["Headline employment rates were still fairly high","the report measures hours/underemployment rather than earnings or poverty directly"],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.10"]}

- The World Bank's December 2022 CEM said development had stagnated, private investment was declining, and Nigeria needed a stronger business-enabling environment and macro stability.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Nigeria Country Economic Memorandum: Charting a New Course","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/country/nigeria/publication/nigeria-economic-update-charting-a-new-course","claim":"The World Bank said development had stagnated over the last decade, private investment was declining, and Nigeria needed macro stability, exchange-rate reform, lower trade barriers, higher non-oil revenues, and fixes to power, finance, and digitalization constraints.","locator":"lines 38-45","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official World Bank diagnosis with concrete growth and investment constraints.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact late-2022 assessment.","ruler_attribution":"Mostly regime-level and structural, but highly relevant to Buhari's final-year economic stewardship.","contrary_evidence":["The memo covers the prior decade, not only 2022","it identifies structural and inherited constraints"],"lenses":["5B.4","5B.6","5B.7","5B.10"]}

- Buhari ordered ministers and other appointees with political ambitions to resign, then reassigned ministers to reinvigorate sectors.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Serve the Nation Diligently, President Buhari Charges Newly Appointed Ministers; Re-assigns Four Others","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-07-06","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/serve-the-nation-diligently-president-buhari-charges-newly-appointed-ministers-re-assigns-four-others/","claim":"Buhari assigned portfolios to seven newly appointed ministers, said the redeployment was meant to reinvigorate sectors where better results were desired, and told the new team to serve diligently and focus on key programs.","locator":"lines 27-30","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential announcement of cabinet changes and purpose.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 cabinet-management period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari cabinet choice and administrative direction.","contrary_evidence":["The speech is generic and does not prove competence","the reshuffle is not itself evidence of outcomes"],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.6","5B.7"]}

- Buhari also ordered ministers and appointees seeking office to resign, presenting cabinet exit as part of a clean separation from politics.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"President Buhari to Outgoing Ministers: Significant Number of You Sufficiently Equipped to Be My Successor","publisher":"The State House, Abuja","publication_date":"2022-05-13","url":"https://statehouse.gov.ng/president-buhari-to-outgoing-ministers-significant-number-of-you-sufficiently-equipped-to-be-my-successor/","claim":"Buhari said ministers and political appointees seeking elective office should resign their current appointments, said replacements would be made without delay, and described the cabinet departure as a normal transition.","locator":"lines 29-36 and 56-63","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct presidential directive and cabinet-management statement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 pre-election period.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari action on appointments and entrenchment risk.","contrary_evidence":["Several ministers were themselves seeking office, so the cabinet was not apolitical","this does not resolve broader CBN politicization"],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5","5B.7"]}

- ICIR reported that CBN governor Godwin Emefiele became politicized in 2022 by pursuing a presidential ambition while still in office, and that the finance minister was not informed about the naira-redesign plan.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Godwin Emefiele: CBN governor in the eye of the storm","publisher":"International Centre for Investigative Reporting","publication_date":"2022-12-22","url":"https://www.icirnigeria.org/cbn-governor-in-the-eye-of-the-storm/","claim":"ICIR said Emefiele's 2022 presidential ambition became a major controversy, that he announced it after meeting Buhari, and that Finance Minister Zainab Ahmed said she had not been informed about the naira-redesign plan.","locator":"lines 376-379 and 423-427","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Investigative local reporting with concrete contemporaneous details and named participants.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 politicization episode.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but materially attributable to Buhari's administration because the CBN governor operated under his tenure and met with him before the announcement.","contrary_evidence":["The piece is retrospective and investigative rather than a formal inquiry","it reflects controversy and capture risk more than a full causal finding"],"lenses":["5B.2","5B.5","5B.7"]}

- Buhari and the CBN framed the naira redesign as anti-corruption and anti-intimidation policy, with a cashless-economy rationale.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Buhari unveils redesigned naira notes","publisher":"TheCable","publication_date":"2022-11-23","url":"https://www.thecable.ng/breaking-buhari-unveils-redesigned-naira-notes/","claim":"Buhari said the redesigned naira would help address illicit financial flows, corruption, the economy, and the value of the currency, while the CBN said the policy would help control money supply and support monetary policy.","locator":"lines 79-95","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous reporting of a formal policy unveiling with direct quotes from Buhari and Emefiele.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 policy launch.","ruler_attribution":"Direct Buhari and CBN policy intent.","contrary_evidence":["The policy later triggered cash-scarcity problems","intent to fight corruption does not prove effective implementation"],"lenses":["5B.1","5B.4","5B.7","5B.8"]}

- Reuters reported that the new naira notes were in short supply at launch and many Nigerians, especially those in rural and informal settings, thought the deadline was too tight.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New Nigerian naira notes a rare sight on first day of circulation","publisher":"Reuters","publication_date":"2022-12-16","url":"https://www.marketscreener.com/news/latest/New-Nigerian-naira-notes-a-rare-sight-on-first-day-of-circulation-42566251/","claim":"Reuters said the redesigned naira notes were in short supply on the first day of circulation, while many Nigerians said the deadline was too tight, especially for rural and informal-market users.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reporting with clear contemporaneous market and household impact, albeit via a republication page.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact launch period in December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Directly linked to Buhari's approved policy, though the operational failure was executed by the CBN.","contrary_evidence":["This is launch-day evidence, not a full medium-run evaluation","the policy aim was anti-corruption and cash control"],"lenses":["5B.8","5B.9","5B.10"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across official budget documents, IMF Article IV materials, World Bank reviews/memos, NBS inflation and labor releases, State House notices, ICIR investigative reporting, Reuters-based naira-redesign coverage, local media on cash policy, and a few Reuters/Reuters-in-News duplicate republications.

Kept sources were chosen because they added distinct claims on: budget intent and execution, subsidy/fiscal drag, revenue and tax reform, inflation, labor-market distribution, private investment and business conditions, cabinet/appointment quality, and the naira redesign's intended and actual effects.

Rejected or downgraded material included:
- ReutersConnect image-only or licensable-photo pages with no substantive text.
- Duplicate republications of the same Reuters story on the naira redesign and cabinet resignations.
- Out-of-period 2023 retrospectives that added no new 2022-specific facts beyond what 2022 or early-2023 official releases already showed.
- Generic country overview pages and local priors already captured in the structured evidence package.
- Opinion or explainer pieces that repeated the same cash-redesign arguments without adding materially new claims.

**Remaining gaps**
1. Direct Buhari-attributed evidence on 5B.2 is still thinner than for macro/fiscal policy. The cabinet reshuffle and the Emefiele politicization episode help, but there is still not much clean evidence on whether Buhari systematically appointed economic professionals or over-relied on loyalists in the core economic team.
2. I found stronger evidence on subsidies, inflation, borrowing, and investment than on entrepreneurship/property-rights reform in a narrow sense. The business-environment evidence is mostly macro-level.
3. Household-level welfare evidence is decent for distribution, but earnings and consumption outcomes would benefit from one more targeted source if you want a sharper view of 5B.8.
4. The naira-redesign episode gives both intent and adverse implementation evidence, but the causal chain between Buhari's intent and the later cash crunch is still partly mediated by the CBN's execution. More direct implementation documentation would sharpen attribution.
